WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.835
-10 -- count 'em --
10 Democratic candidates

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:04.237
squared off tonight
in the first debate

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:05.438
of the presidential campaign.

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:06.873
For more on this,
it's time for "A Closer Look.

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:11.845
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:14.213
Donald Trump is
constitutionally incapable

00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:16.015
of sharing the spotlight
with anyone,

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:18.084
and so as 10 Democrats
prepared to take the stage

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:19.552
for their first
primary debate tonight,

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:22.588
he went to something called
the Faith and Freedom Conference

00:00:22.588 --> 00:00:25.324
to gurgle up a bunch of
old-man nonsense.

00:00:25.324 --> 00:00:26.592
-We're saying
"merry Christmas" again.

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:27.693
Did you notice? Remember?

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:31.197
[ Cheers and applause ]
Remember?

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:33.299
I usually save that for
November, December,

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:34.600
but I was just thinking...

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.503
They were all taking it down off
the department stores,

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:37.836
everything.

00:00:37.836 --> 00:00:41.073
You'd see a big red --
They'd say, "Happy holidays,"

00:00:41.073 --> 00:00:42.141
no "merry Christmas."

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:43.609
They're saying
"merry Christmas" again.

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:44.844
It's very interesting.

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:49.849
"Our president may not be
the best at the Bible.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:54.987
He may not have read it
2,000 times.

00:00:54.987 --> 00:00:57.090
But he's the best for us."

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.559
My wife watched.
I said, "You have to see this.

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:03.562
It's great invention
It's called TiVo," okay?

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:05.398
I don't want to be advertising,
but, you know,

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:07.333
it's, like,
better than television,

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:08.801
'cause television, you
never see it.

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:11.437
With TiVo, you play it back.
I played it back.

00:01:11.437 --> 00:01:15.741
-Oh, my God!
Get me off this cruise ship!

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:18.845
Is this a presidential speech
or bingo night?

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:22.148
This guy shouldn't be standing
behind a presidential seal.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:24.417
He should be wandering down
the hallway of a group home

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.386
with a nurse chasing
after him, screaming,

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:29.522
"Donald, your gown is
on backwards."

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:35.061
I'm sorry.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:38.430
You've just heard about TiVo?

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.801
Trump's brain is so
permanently stuck in 1999.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:44.170
"Have you heard this new food
they just came out with?

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.473
It's called Go-Gurt.

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:49.942
It's a game changer."

00:01:49.942 --> 00:01:51.944
Trump was probably more off
kilter than usual today

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:53.112
with the Democratic
debate looming

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:54.781
and the news that
Robert Mueller's agreed

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:56.682
to testify in public before
Congress next month,

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:57.683
and it clearly...

00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:59.752
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:02.421
...clearly got in Trump's head,
because he called up

00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:04.924
Fox Business News to
and ramble about Mueller

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:06.792
and accuse him of
a totally made-up crime.

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:09.195
Trump was referring to unearthed
text messages between

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:10.963
two FBI agents
who were having an affair,

00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:13.466
or at least I think that's what
he was referring to.

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:16.202
-Robert Mueller --
they worked for him,

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.270
and the two lovers were together

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.472
and they had
texts back and forth.

00:02:20.472 --> 00:02:22.441
-Well, how come
we haven't seen it all?

00:02:22.441 --> 00:02:23.977
-You know why?

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:27.279
Because Mueller
terminated them illegally.

00:02:27.279 --> 00:02:29.248
He terminated the e-mails.

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:32.584
He terminated all of the stuff
between Strzok and Page.

00:02:32.584 --> 00:02:34.653
You know, they --
You've never --

00:02:34.653 --> 00:02:39.158
Robert Mueller terminated
their text messages together.

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:42.261
He would -- He terminated them.
They're gone.

00:02:42.261 --> 00:02:43.963
And that's illegal.

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:46.499
-Deleted!

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:50.503
The word you're looking for
is "deleted."

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:54.440
My God, man,
go back to watching TiVo.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.475
And now you're accusing
Robert Mueller of a crime?

00:02:56.475 --> 00:02:57.610
Look at this guy.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.312
I doubt he's ever jaywalked
in his life.

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:01.147
Robert Mueller gives
speeding tickets

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:05.150
by chasing after cars on foot.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:06.986
And no matter what you think of
the Democrats,

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:09.855
this is why it was refreshing to
see sane human beings

00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:12.392
speaking in coherent sentences
about actual policies

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:13.959
on national television
for a change.

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:16.028
Now, this was just
the first of two debates,

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:17.964
with 10 more candidates
scheduled to debate on Thursday.

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:19.331
In fact, there are now
a total of

00:03:19.331 --> 00:03:22.001
24 Democrats
running for president.

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:24.337
Look at how many people
are on that screen.

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.672
There are so many people,
you didn't even notice

00:03:26.672 --> 00:03:30.709
that one of them is Guy Fieri.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:33.613
And...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:37.016
And he's definitely more
qualified than Donald Trump,

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:41.521
because, need I remind you,
he's the mayor of Flavortown.

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:45.457
Great bowling alleys,
terrible schools.

00:03:45.457 --> 00:03:48.627
Of course...
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.627 --> 00:03:51.997
We'll just --
We'll cut out that.

00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:55.335
Cut out the Flavortown joke
I fought for all day.

00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:56.469
Live?!

00:03:56.469 --> 00:04:00.873
Of course, with 10 people
on the stage,

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:03.142
the candidates knew it
would be tough to stand out,

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:05.344
which is why they were all
preparing in different ways

00:04:05.344 --> 00:04:08.447
to make their case as concise
and effective as possible.

00:04:08.447 --> 00:04:11.150
-Senator Elizabeth Warren
and her 2020 Democratic rivals

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.185
are very busy right now,
because they're prepping for

00:04:13.185 --> 00:04:15.154
the first pair of
Democratic debates.

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:17.957
Warren has focussed on boiling
down her positions,

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:21.060
her policy positions, to try to
fit the time constraints.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.297
Senator Amy Klobuchar has spent
time watching the 2016 debates

00:04:25.297 --> 00:04:28.067
to see how President Trump
and his GOP rivals

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:30.169
stood out in that crowded field.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:32.905
-Wait. You're studying Trump's
debate performances

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:34.273
to see how he stood out?

00:04:34.273 --> 00:04:35.607
Which ones did you watch?

00:04:35.607 --> 00:04:37.777
Did you study the tape of that
time Trump and Ben Carson

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.545
missed their cues
and got stuck in the hallway

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:41.847
leading to the debate stage?

00:04:41.847 --> 00:04:45.718
'Cause that is a case
where he stood out literally.

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:48.354
And it really is amazing that
out of everyone on the stage,

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.622
the two guys who ended up
in the federal government

00:04:50.622 --> 00:04:52.858
are the ones who turned
a presidential debate

00:04:52.858 --> 00:04:55.261
into an escape room.

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:58.464
"Help us, clue master.
We need a hint."

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.266
Seriously, what lessons
can you learn

00:05:00.266 --> 00:05:01.434
from Trump's
debate performances?

00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:03.002
"Okay, just remember,
yell as loud as you can,

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:04.737
limit your vocabulary
to four words,

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:06.973
and, if you get lost,
just remember to act natural

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:10.242
and stare directly into
the camera.

00:05:10.242 --> 00:05:12.778
like you're having
your portrait drawn."

00:05:12.778 --> 00:05:16.248
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:16.248 --> 00:05:21.286
Now, before the debate,
the candidates also showed up

00:05:21.286 --> 00:05:24.156
to check out the debate stage,
like Ohio Congressman Tim Ryan,

00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:26.859
who got caught sporting
a very casual look.

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:29.061
-2020 contender Tim Ryan is on
the debate stage right now,

00:05:29.061 --> 00:05:30.496
doing his walk-through.

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:33.065
That's going to be happening
through the course of the day.

00:05:33.065 --> 00:05:35.567
We do have a presidential
candidate right with us.

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:38.804
There's Tim Ryan
wearing his cap backward.

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:43.442
-"Uh...

00:05:43.442 --> 00:05:48.747
Hey, guys, there aren't cameras
here now, right?"

00:05:48.747 --> 00:05:50.149
Did you get confused and think

00:05:50.149 --> 00:05:52.718
this was your
office softball game?

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:54.353
You don't look like a candidate.

00:05:54.353 --> 00:05:55.554
You look like you should be
pounding some

00:05:55.554 --> 00:05:59.058
Sam Adams tall-boys
at a Red Sox game.

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:00.392
Now, say what you will
about the Democrats.

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:03.095
Most of them seemed to enjoy
talking about policies,

00:06:03.095 --> 00:06:06.132
and the campaign so far has been
incredibly substantive.

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:07.633
Senator Kamala Harris has a plan

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:10.370
for cash payments to
middle-class household.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:12.038
Senator Bernie Sanders
rolled out a plan

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:13.439
to cancel all student debt.

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:15.307
And Senator Elizabeth Warren
was so excited

00:06:15.307 --> 00:06:17.643
to discuss her plans to tax
the wealthy at a town hall

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:19.345
that she could
hardly contain herself.

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:21.347
-Thank you, Senator Warren,
for being here.

00:06:21.347 --> 00:06:22.949
How the do you plan to make sure

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:25.318
the extremely rich pay
their fair taxes?

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:26.586
-Whoa!

00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:30.289
-Whoa!

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.925
Taxes!

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:39.865
That question was
Elizabeth Warren's Mardi Gras.

00:06:39.865 --> 00:06:42.201
Asking Warren that question
is like walking up

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:43.970
to Chris Cuomo
at a party and going,

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:48.106
"Hey, you know anything
about CrossFit?"

00:06:48.106 --> 00:06:50.142
And that excitement
for talking about policy

00:06:50.142 --> 00:06:52.411
seems to have caught on, because
while the polls

00:06:52.411 --> 00:06:55.415
have stayed mostly flat so far,
Warren's stock has been rising,

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:56.882
and that's caught
the attention of both

00:06:56.882 --> 00:06:59.551
the Democratic front-runner,
Joe Biden, and President Trump.

00:06:59.551 --> 00:07:02.387
-New polling shows Warren's
economic message

00:07:02.387 --> 00:07:04.289
is resonating with voters.

00:07:04.289 --> 00:07:06.859
In the latest NBC News/"Wall
Street Journal" poll,

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:10.529
26% of Democratic primary voters
say they're enthusiastic

00:07:10.529 --> 00:07:12.064
about her candidacy.

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:14.800
That's a 6-point bump
from last month.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.570
It might explain why Biden's
team is also reportedly

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.406
getting a little worried
about her rise.

00:07:20.406 --> 00:07:23.875
And President Trump's campaign
is zeroing in on

00:07:23.875 --> 00:07:25.945
the threat that she could pose.

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.746
-Oh, come on.
Donald Trump doesn't zero in.

00:07:27.746 --> 00:07:31.016
If anything, he zeros out.

00:07:31.016 --> 00:07:32.584
And I especially can't imagine
him zeroing in

00:07:32.584 --> 00:07:33.786
on Elizabeth Warren.

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:35.287
For one thing, Warren literally
did an interview

00:07:35.287 --> 00:07:37.890
where she was running to catch
a train at Penn Station.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.092
Can you imagine if Trump was
late for a train?

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:41.326
Forget answering questions.

00:07:41.326 --> 00:07:43.595
Mike Pence would have to carry
him on his back

00:07:43.595 --> 00:07:46.466
like a tired 4-year-old.

00:07:46.466 --> 00:07:48.734
So, with Warren
the obvious standout

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:50.669
coming into this debate,
the other candidates

00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:51.837
were looking for ways to shine,

00:07:51.837 --> 00:07:53.673
and former Texas Congressman
Beto O'Rourke

00:07:53.673 --> 00:07:56.508
settled on a strategy of going
bilingual right out of the gate.

00:07:56.508 --> 00:08:00.045
-This economy
has got to work for everyone,

00:08:00.045 --> 00:08:02.514
and, right now,
we know that it isn't.

00:08:02.514 --> 00:08:04.283
And it's going to take
all of us coming together

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:06.385
to make sure that it does.

00:08:06.385 --> 00:08:08.421
[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:13.959
-I mean, I knew he was going to
bust out his Spanish.

00:08:13.959 --> 00:08:16.095
I just didn't know
it would be that quickly.

00:08:16.095 --> 00:08:17.863
Remember, Beto, you only
have one bullet,

00:08:17.863 --> 00:08:21.667
so don't fire until you see the
whites of their -- Bang!

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:23.402
Also, check out
Cory Booker's face

00:08:23.402 --> 00:08:25.137
when it cuts to him during
Beto's answer.

00:08:25.137 --> 00:08:27.173
-[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:08:33.078 --> 00:08:38.617
-Now, you might think that's
a face of a guy thinking,

00:08:38.617 --> 00:08:40.986
"I can't believe you're
pandering by speaking Spanish,"

00:08:40.986 --> 00:08:42.521
but it's actually the face of
a guy thinking,

00:08:42.521 --> 00:08:43.522
"Oh, man!

00:08:43.522 --> 00:08:44.723
I was gonna do that."

00:08:44.723 --> 00:08:46.759
-[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:08:55.668 --> 00:08:58.070
-Not only do I applaud them
speaking Spanish,

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.172
you just know everyone in
tomorrow's debate

00:09:00.172 --> 00:09:01.574
who doesn't speak Spanish

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:04.276
is going to spend
the whole day cramming.

00:09:04.276 --> 00:09:06.879
"I think all Americans
want answered --

00:09:06.879 --> 00:09:08.447
the question they
want answered is,

00:09:08.447 --> 00:09:11.784
'¿Donde esta mi gato?'"

00:09:11.784 --> 00:09:16.355
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:21.527
Now, Beto has been
struggling in the polls,

00:09:21.527 --> 00:09:23.061
despite the national fame
he gained

00:09:23.061 --> 00:09:24.763
from his Senate campaign
against Ted Cruz,

00:09:24.763 --> 00:09:27.566
but he was still head
and shoulder above some rivals

00:09:27.566 --> 00:09:30.670
in name and face recognition,
like, um...this guy.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:33.972
-Congressman Delaney,
do you agree?

00:09:33.972 --> 00:09:37.409
-I think we need do real things
to help American workers

00:09:37.409 --> 00:09:38.511
and the American people.

00:09:38.511 --> 00:09:41.948
-Okay, quick question --
who are you?

00:09:41.948 --> 00:09:43.516
You look like you were
supposed to be in

00:09:43.516 --> 00:09:45.817
a lawn-care commercial
during the break,

00:09:45.817 --> 00:09:49.121
but they just accidentally
let you stay.

00:09:49.121 --> 00:09:52.190
And then there was Tim Ryan,
the Ohio congressman,

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:53.759
who seemed maybe
slightly surprised

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:54.894
that he got a question.

00:09:54.894 --> 00:09:56.428
-Congressman Ryan,
President Trump --

00:09:56.428 --> 00:09:59.098
and you just referred to him --
promised that

00:09:59.098 --> 00:10:02.768
manufacturing jobs
were all coming back to places

00:10:02.768 --> 00:10:05.036
like your home state of Ohio.

00:10:05.036 --> 00:10:07.873
Can you make that same promise?

00:10:07.873 --> 00:10:09.909
-Yes, I believe you can.

00:10:09.909 --> 00:10:14.313
-Tim Ryan always looks like
his dog just talked.

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:23.021
"Rusty, did you just say,
'Good morning'?"

00:10:23.021 --> 00:10:24.823
Then the debate moderators
gave the candidates

00:10:24.823 --> 00:10:26.225
a chance to
differentiate themselves

00:10:26.225 --> 00:10:27.460
with a key policy question --

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:29.227
who would abolish
private health insurance?

00:10:29.227 --> 00:10:31.296
And only a few
raised their hands.

00:10:31.296 --> 00:10:34.366
-Who here would abolish their
private health insurance

00:10:34.366 --> 00:10:36.335
in favor of
a government-run plan?

00:10:36.335 --> 00:10:41.807
Just a show of hands
to start off with.

00:10:41.807 --> 00:10:43.141
-Not only did Warren
raise her hand,

00:10:43.141 --> 00:10:45.411
but check out New York City
mayor Bill de Blasio.

00:10:45.411 --> 00:10:47.113
He's got to be so happy
he raised his hand

00:10:47.113 --> 00:10:48.280
when Warren did.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:49.915
It's like when
you're taking the SATs

00:10:49.915 --> 00:10:51.550
and you peek at
the valedictorian's test

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:54.419
and make sure she had
the same answer.

00:10:54.419 --> 00:10:55.821
And de Blasio clearly said
it's an opportunity

00:10:55.821 --> 00:10:57.522
to make a name for himself
on the healthcare issue,

00:10:57.522 --> 00:10:59.091
because after Beto
answered the question,

00:10:59.091 --> 00:11:00.793
de Blasio jumped in
and went after him.

00:11:00.793 --> 00:11:02.994
-Would you replace
private insurance?

00:11:02.994 --> 00:11:04.964
-No. I think the choice is
fundamental to --

00:11:04.964 --> 00:11:06.265
-Wait, wait.

00:11:06.265 --> 00:11:09.335
Congressman O'Rourke,
private insurance is not working

00:11:09.335 --> 00:11:11.103
for ten of
millions of Americans.

00:11:11.103 --> 00:11:13.506
When you talk about the co-pays,
the deductibles,

00:11:13.506 --> 00:11:15.541
the premiums,
the out-of-pocket expenses,

00:11:15.541 --> 00:11:16.942
it's not working.

00:11:16.942 --> 00:11:19.744
-That's right, so for those for
whom it is not working,

00:11:19.744 --> 00:11:21.480
they can choose Medicare.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.283
-Congressman, you've got to
start by acknowledging

00:11:24.283 --> 00:11:25.884
the system is
not working for people.

00:11:25.884 --> 00:11:27.352
-Look at those two.

00:11:27.352 --> 00:11:31.390
They look like stockbrokers who
got too drunk at happy hour.

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:33.058
It's so weird to watch
two guys get into it

00:11:33.058 --> 00:11:34.659
who are both polling
in single digits.

00:11:34.659 --> 00:11:36.628
It's like watching the
New York Jets play guys

00:11:36.628 --> 00:11:39.598
who got cut from
the New York Jets.

00:11:39.598 --> 00:11:42.134
De Blasio was so eager to get
a word in that, at one point,

00:11:42.134 --> 00:11:43.368
as they were cutting to
commercial,

00:11:43.368 --> 00:11:44.903
he started yelling over
the moderators.

00:11:44.903 --> 00:11:47.839
-Put the American people first.
-Hey, but wait a minute.

00:11:47.839 --> 00:11:48.841
-All right, we are out of time.

00:11:48.841 --> 00:11:50.175
We're up against a hard break --

00:11:50.175 --> 00:11:53.312
-Neither Democrats, nor
Republicans have been serious --

00:11:53.312 --> 00:11:54.579
-Mayor de Blasio,
we'll have more.

00:11:54.579 --> 00:11:56.915
The commercial is coming.

00:11:56.915 --> 00:11:58.183
-Oh, man, that's so New York.

00:11:58.183 --> 00:11:59.785
It's like when you're trying
to talk to someone

00:11:59.785 --> 00:12:01.287
as the subway doors are closing.

00:12:01.287 --> 00:12:05.390
"Hey, what did you call me?!
What did you say, pal?!"

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:07.626
Of course,
I'm referring to a time

00:12:07.626 --> 00:12:10.195
when the subway doors
in this city closed.

00:12:10.195 --> 00:12:14.165
So, it was a very substantive
issue-oriented debate.

00:12:14.165 --> 00:12:16.501
And after about an hour full of
complex policy questions

00:12:16.501 --> 00:12:17.836
about everything
from immigration

00:12:17.836 --> 00:12:19.872
to gun control to healthcare,
the president decided

00:12:19.872 --> 00:12:22.808
to weigh in with his own
contribution to the debate,

00:12:22.808 --> 00:12:25.410
tweeting, "Boring."

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:26.512
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:12:26.512 --> 00:12:30.682
Are you not entertained,
Caligula?

00:12:30.682 --> 00:12:33.251
I'd say we should send you out
there to spice things up,

00:12:33.251 --> 00:12:36.121
but you'd probably just get
stuck in the hallway.

00:12:36.121 --> 00:12:39.158
"This escape room is boring."

00:12:39.158 --> 00:12:41.160
Also, don't you realize that
most Americans

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.929
are dying for things
to be boring for a while?

00:12:43.929 --> 00:12:46.297
I fantasize about the day
I turn on CNN

00:12:46.297 --> 00:12:50.202
and there's a banner that says,
"No breaking news."

00:12:50.202 --> 00:12:51.837
Things were also a little weird
when the debate

00:12:51.837 --> 00:12:53.071
switched over to the second hour

00:12:53.071 --> 00:12:55.273
for moderators Rachel Maddow
and Chuck Todd,

00:12:55.273 --> 00:12:57.375
who decided to start out with
some friendly banter.

00:12:57.375 --> 00:12:59.778
-Now, the candidates will have
60 seconds to answer,

00:12:59.778 --> 00:13:02.147
30 seconds for a follow-up,
if necessary.

00:13:02.147 --> 00:13:04.550
And we will be ruthless,
if necessary.

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:05.784
-Yeah, we can do that.
-Mm-hmm.

00:13:05.784 --> 00:13:07.252
-By the way, hi, Rachel.
-Hi, Chuck.

00:13:07.252 --> 00:13:08.386
-How you doing?
-Good.

00:13:08.386 --> 00:13:10.422
-Hey, guys,
we don't need the hellos.

00:13:10.422 --> 00:13:12.758
We know you know each other.

00:13:12.758 --> 00:13:15.861
The candidates each get like
9 seconds to speak,

00:13:15.861 --> 00:13:17.929
and you're chatting it up
like old college friends

00:13:17.929 --> 00:13:20.566
who ran into each other
at Fuddruckers.

00:13:20.566 --> 00:13:22.034
It's like they
were waiting for somebody

00:13:22.034 --> 00:13:25.337
to come by and take their order.

00:13:25.337 --> 00:13:27.206
But then once they got
the hellos out of the way,

00:13:27.206 --> 00:13:29.107
they were ready to go
with a smooth transition

00:13:29.107 --> 00:13:30.843
in the next portion
of the debate.

00:13:30.843 --> 00:13:32.243
-Many people are calling for
a restoration

00:13:32.243 --> 00:13:35.814
of an assault-weapons ban,
but even if implemented,

00:13:35.814 --> 00:13:37.650
there will still be
hundreds of millions of guns

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:38.983
in this country.

00:13:38.983 --> 00:13:40.919
Should there be a role for the
federal government --

00:13:40.919 --> 00:13:43.555
-I think the other mics are on.
-Oh, everybody's mics are on.

00:13:43.555 --> 00:13:45.958
I think we have a --
I heard that, too.

00:13:45.958 --> 00:13:47.192
That's okay.

00:13:47.192 --> 00:13:49.194
I think we had a little
mic issue in the back.

00:13:49.194 --> 00:13:50.896
-Control room, we've got --

00:13:50.896 --> 00:13:53.432
-I think we heard -- Yeah,
we have the audience audio.

00:13:53.432 --> 00:13:54.566
-What's happening?

00:13:54.566 --> 00:13:56.735
-We are hearing
our colleagues's audio.

00:13:56.735 --> 00:13:59.504
If the control room
can turn off the mics.

00:13:59.504 --> 00:14:01.640
-There's voices behind us.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:03.609
-Yeah, if the control room
can turn off

00:14:03.609 --> 00:14:05.577
the mics of our previous
moderators, we will --

00:14:05.577 --> 00:14:08.313
-I think it's the
prior moderators.

00:14:08.313 --> 00:14:11.684
-You know,
we've prepared for everything.

00:14:11.684 --> 00:14:12.885
We did not prepare for this.

00:14:12.885 --> 00:14:14.686
-We are going to take
a quick break.

00:14:14.686 --> 00:14:18.323
We're gonna get
this technical situation fixed.

00:14:18.323 --> 00:14:19.624
We will be right back.

00:14:19.624 --> 00:14:21.159
-Maybe you guys should have
spent more time

00:14:21.159 --> 00:14:24.863
checking the audio and less time
saying hello to each other.

00:14:24.863 --> 00:14:27.132
I'm surprised Warren
didn't bust out a tool kit.

00:14:27.132 --> 00:14:30.302
"I can fix it."

00:14:30.302 --> 00:14:37.442
And, of course, our
TV-game-show-host president,

00:14:37.442 --> 00:14:39.411
who has nothing to say about
policy, had plenty to say

00:14:39.411 --> 00:14:42.381
about the technical glitch,
tweeting, "NBC News and MSNBC

00:14:42.381 --> 00:14:43.849
should be ashamed of themselves
for having

00:14:43.849 --> 00:14:45.584
such a horrible
technical breakdown

00:14:45.584 --> 00:14:46.652
in the middle of the debate.

00:14:46.652 --> 00:14:48.420
Truly unprofessional
and only worthy

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.490
of a fake-news organization
which they are."

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:54.192
Boring!

00:14:54.192 --> 00:14:56.594
Hey, man, I wouldn't be tweeting
about technical glitches.

00:14:56.594 --> 00:14:59.398
You think TiVo is
new technology.

00:14:59.398 --> 00:15:01.366
"NBC needs better
audio equipment.

00:15:01.366 --> 00:15:04.636
They should have used a Zune."

00:15:04.636 --> 00:15:06.171
Although, you know
he was TiVo'ing

00:15:06.171 --> 00:15:07.405
that moment multiple times.

00:15:07.405 --> 00:15:08.473
"Melania, get in here and watch

00:15:08.473 --> 00:15:10.142
sleepy Chuck Todd
get interrupted."

00:15:10.142 --> 00:15:12.511
Bloop-bloop.

00:15:12.511 --> 00:15:15.046
But a big question underlying
all these policy debates

00:15:15.046 --> 00:15:16.982
is this -- how will you get
your agenda passed

00:15:16.982 --> 00:15:18.317
if there's a
Republican Congress?

00:15:18.317 --> 00:15:20.252
The moderators asked Warren
if she had any ideas

00:15:20.252 --> 00:15:23.054
for dealing with that,
and her answer was unequivocal.

00:15:23.054 --> 00:15:25.190
-Do you have a plan to deal with
with Mitch McConnell?

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:30.729
-I do.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:15:30.729 --> 00:15:32.564
-Damn!

00:15:32.564 --> 00:15:35.634
If Mitch McConnell were capable
of feeling human emotion,

00:15:35.634 --> 00:15:38.069
he'd be terrified right now.

00:15:38.069 --> 00:15:39.637
That's the moment everyone
else wondered,

00:15:39.637 --> 00:15:41.139
"Maybe I should just go."

00:15:41.139 --> 00:15:43.909
That was like when the matriarch
of a Southern dynasty

00:15:43.909 --> 00:15:45.711
stands up at
the dinner table and says,

00:15:45.711 --> 00:15:48.347
"I've made a decision."

00:15:48.347 --> 00:15:50.115
When Warren said that,
Booker's face

00:15:50.115 --> 00:15:51.349
got permanently stuck like that,

00:15:51.349 --> 00:15:56.889
and Tim Ryan's hat
got flew back onto his head.

00:15:56.889 --> 00:15:58.390
Everyone was eager to
get a word in,

00:15:58.390 --> 00:15:59.924
like Washington governor
Jay Inslee,

00:15:59.924 --> 00:16:02.260
who's running a campaign focused
primarily on climate change.

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:03.962
He tried to get some attention
from the moderators

00:16:03.962 --> 00:16:05.430
and then made it kind of weird.

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:07.532
-Thank you, Senator Booker.
Rachel's got the next question.

00:16:07.532 --> 00:16:09.200
-We are going to make --
We are going -- Hold on.

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:11.537
Governor, you're gonna be happy
with where we go.

00:16:11.537 --> 00:16:15.474
Governor Inslee,
this next question is to you.

00:16:15.474 --> 00:16:16.642
You got me?
-Rachel --

00:16:16.642 --> 00:16:17.910
-You got me?

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:20.079
-Oh, my God.
What does that mean?

00:16:20.079 --> 00:16:21.146
"I'm keeping an eye on her"?

00:16:21.146 --> 00:16:23.448
He's like the corny dad
at the party

00:16:23.448 --> 00:16:25.517
who wants to make sure
everyone's had enough to drink.

00:16:25.517 --> 00:16:27.485
"Chad, I'm onto you, buddy.

00:16:27.485 --> 00:16:30.989
When that marg's empty,
I'm getting you a refill, Chad."

00:16:30.989 --> 00:16:32.991
Ultimately, tonight's debate
showed us that

00:16:32.991 --> 00:16:36.028
a substantive campaign focused
on issues people care about,

00:16:36.028 --> 00:16:38.096
rather than incoherent rambling
about Christmas

00:16:38.096 --> 00:16:40.398
or terminating e-mails
is possible,

00:16:40.398 --> 00:16:41.667
but it also proved once again

00:16:41.667 --> 00:16:43.001
that there's
a reason "Jeopardy!"

00:16:43.001 --> 00:16:47.005
has three contestants
and not 10.

00:16:47.005 --> 00:16:48.273
"Alex, Alex, call on me!

00:16:48.273 --> 00:16:50.375
I have a Final Jeopardy!
answer."

00:16:50.375 --> 00:16:51.643
And if you missed
tonight's debate,

00:16:51.643 --> 00:16:53.645
don't worry -- the president has
a high-tech solution

00:16:53.645 --> 00:16:55.013
for how you can watch it.

00:16:55.013 --> 00:16:57.482
-"It's great invention
It's called TiVo."

00:16:57.482 --> 00:16:58.684
-This has been "A Closer Look."

00:16:58.684 --> 00:17:02.354
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

